@* Section VI — Regulation & Discipline Processes *@
<p>
    The final section covers how the profession holds members and firms to account — worth about
    <strong>10% (7–10 questions)</strong>.
</p>

<h2 id="discipline"><span class="rref">VI.1</span> Discipline Procedures</h2>
<p><strong>Enforcement</strong> targets unlicensed people who engage in engineering or geoscience, use protected titles, provide licensed services without a Permit to Practice, or stamp documents with a professional seal. The <strong>Complaints Committee</strong> investigates complaints from the public or members, contacts the accused, and can escalate to the Discipline Committee.</p>
<p><strong>Discipline</strong> applies to licensed members who commit:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Professional wrongdoing or carelessness</li>
    <li>Lack of skill or failure to meet professional standards</li>
    <li>Dishonesty or ethical violations</li>
    <li>Physical or mental incapacity</li>
    <li>Conviction of serious offences or other unprofessional conduct</li>
</ul>
<p>The disciplinary process has three stages: <strong>collecting evidence</strong>, <strong>investigation and peer review</strong>, and <strong>hearings</strong>. Penalties the Discipline Committee may impose include:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Suspending or revoking a licence, permit, or certificate of authorization</li>
    <li>Charging investigation and hearing expenses</li>
    <li>Requiring education (a training program or test)</li>
    <li>Publishing results (in the case of revocation)</li>
    <li>Fines</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout">
    <span class="clabel">How a complaint flows</span>
    <p>
        Complaints go in writing to the <strong>Registrar</strong>, who may resolve them through education or
        mediation. The <strong>Investigative Committee</strong> evaluates that agreement, runs a preliminary
        investigation, and can escalate to the <strong>Discipline Committee</strong>, which holds a formal
        inquiry and enforces penalties. A <strong>Stipulated Order</strong> is a simpler route for less
        serious matters: a single representative decides guilt or innocence, with no appeal.
    </p>
</div>

<h2 id="review-individuals"><span class="rref">VI.2</span> Practice Review of Individuals</h2>
<p>
    Each year, members of most Associations report their <strong>Continuing Professional Development
    (CPD)</strong> hours. This keeps licensed members current and lets them switch to “non-practising” status
    if needed. Less known: the Association conducts <strong>random inspections</strong> each year on a small
    group of members’ reported information.
</p>

<h2 id="review-firms"><span class="rref">VI.3</span> Practice Review of Firms</h2>
<p>
    All engineering or geoscience providers — companies, partnerships, or corporations — must hold a
    <strong>Permit to Practice</strong>, which guarantees a proper work environment and organizational
    structure. The CEO (or equivalent) holds overall responsibility for that structure, and a senior member
    or full-time employee directs and supervises professional practice. The individual licensed professional
    remains an individual member.
</p>

<h2 id="cpd"><span class="rref">VI.4</span> Continuing Professional Development (CPD)</h2>
<p>Engineers Canada advises Associations to run CPD programs that include stated requirements, annual record-keeping, an annual declaration of compliance, and auditing of a small sample. Activities that count toward CPD include:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Gaining experience on the job</li>
    <li>Formal courses and training programs</li>
    <li>Self-directed learning</li>
    <li>Serving on committees or in mentorship programs</li>
    <li>Preparing and delivering presentations</li>
    <li>Writing papers, patents, or other texts</li>
</ul>
